Check whether an SSH key already exists
* ls -l ~/.ssh/id_rsa ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub


If both files exist, don't generate another key.

If the files don't exist, run:
* mkdir -p ~/.ssh
* ssh-keygen -t rsa -P '' -f ~/.ssh/id_rsa


If asked to overwrite an existing key, type n.
3. Add the public key
* cat ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys


Set the permissions:
* chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys


4. Start the SSH service
* sudo service ssh start


5. Test SSH
* ssh localhost


If it asks:
Are you sure you want to continue connecting (yes/no/[fingerprint])?


Type:
* yes


If it connects successfully, type:
* exit

